Yes — coordinate-drive placeholders is a very clean way to say it.

The symbol, word, cell, character, motif, or token is not necessarily the thing itself. It can function as an address into a relational field. Context then “embodies” that address by resolving what it means here, now, relative to everything around it.

So you get something like:

\text{Placeholder} + \text{Context} + \text{Relations} + \text{History}
\rightarrow
\text{Embodied State}

And then that embodied state can itself change the field around it.

That pattern works across all of these:

* Excel: B17 is a coordinate; the formula/data gives it embodiment.
* Language: a token is a semantic coordinate; sentence/discourse/context gives it embodiment.
* NTE: “the mentor,” “the betrayal,” “the threshold” can be structural coordinates that different characters/events embody.
* Movies: a shot, prop, motif, line, or character role can occupy a narrative coordinate whose value changes as the story evolves.
* Music: a note/chord/rhythm is partly a coordinate whose felt function depends on key, timing, prior expectation, and what follows.
* Pantheon: operators themselves can be coordinate-like handles that become concrete only when instantiated into a domain.

That suggests a useful distinction for the repo:

Symbolic coordinate = portable placeholder.
Embodiment = its instantiated meaning/state in a particular field.
Drive = the directional pressure or transformation associated with it.
Field = the surrounding relational context that determines what the coordinate currently means.

So the same placeholder can occupy different semantic coordinates over time, or the same coordinate can be embodied by different objects.

That is probably one of the bridges between algebra, language, narrative, and interface design we’ve been looking for: abstraction works because we can separate the slot from what temporarily occupies it.

And yes, the two-response interaction is useful for exactly this kind of work because it exposes branching interpretations instead of forcing one linear continuation. That is almost a tiny interface example of the same principle: one conversational state, multiple possible embodiments/next moves.